## How to Avoid Streaky Fall French Tips at Home

Streaky or bulky tip lines on **fall french tip nails** almost always come down to two things: brush angle and polish thickness, rarely a lack of skill. If your liner brush is loaded with too much polish, or you are holding it flat against the nail instead of at a slight angle, the line drags and pools instead of gliding in one clean stroke.

Nail guides or stencils solve the placement half of this problem, giving you a fixed line to paint up to so the smile line stays consistent across all ten nails. But guides do not fix an overloaded brush, which is the actual root cause behind most streaky attempts. Wipe the brush against the bottle neck until it looks almost too dry before starting the tip. It will still lay down enough color.

Two thin coats beat one thick one every time. A single heavy coat of dark polish, especially in colors like black or burgundy, is where bulk and streaking both show up worst, since the polish has nowhere to level out before it starts drying unevenly. Applying two thinner coats, letting each one set for sixty seconds before the next, gives a cleaner, more opaque result with none of the drag marks a single thick coat leaves behind.

With gel polish, cure each coat before layering the next rather than building color in one uncured pass. Skipping this is one of the most common reasons DIY gel tips end up bulky at the free edge, since uncured layers shift and pool right where the tip meets the nail's curve.

